DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These functions are deprecated (and unavailable in glibc).  Use  ggeettaadd‐‐
       ddrriinnffoo(3) and ggeettnnaammeeiinnffoo(3) instead.

       The  ggeettiippnnooddeebbyynnaammee() and ggeettiippnnooddeebbyyaaddddrr() functions return the names
       and addresses of a network host.  These functions return a  pointer  to
       the following structure:

           struct hostent {
               char  *h_name;
               char **h_aliases;
               int    h_addrtype;
               int    h_length;
               char **h_addr_list;
           };

       These functions replace the ggeetthhoossttbbyynnaammee(3) and ggeetthhoossttbbyyaaddddrr(3) func‐
       tions,  which  could  access only the IPv4 network address family.  The
       ggeettiippnnooddeebbyynnaammee() and ggeettiippnnooddeebbyyaaddddrr() functions can  access  multiple
       network address families.

       Unlike  the ggeetthhoossttbbyy functions, these functions return pointers to dy‐
       namically allocated memory.  The ffrreeeehhoosstteenntt() function is used to  re‐
       lease the dynamically allocated memory after the caller no longer needs
       the _h_o_s_t_e_n_t structure.

   ggeettiippnnooddeebbyynnaammee(()) aarrgguummeennttss
       The  ggeettiippnnooddeebbyynnaammee() function looks up network addresses for the host
       specified by the _n_a_m_e argument.  The _a_f argument specifies one  of  the
       following values:

       AAFF__IINNEETT
              The _n_a_m_e argument points to a dotted‐quad IPv4 address or a name
              of an IPv4 network host.

       AAFF__IINNEETT66
              The _n_a_m_e argument points to a hexadecimal IPv6 address or a name
              of an IPv6 network host.

       The  _f_l_a_g_s argument specifies additional options.  More than one option
       can be specified by bitwise OR‐ing them together.  _f_l_a_g_s should be  set
       to 0 if no options are desired.

       AAII__VV44MMAAPPPPEEDD
              This  flag is used with AAFF__IINNEETT66 to request a query for IPv4 ad‐
              dresses instead of IPv6 addresses; the IPv4  addresses  will  be
              mapped to IPv6 addresses.

       AAII__AALLLL This  flag  is used with AAII__VV44MMAAPPPPEEDD to request a query for both
              IPv4 and IPv6 addresses.  Any IPv4 address found will be  mapped
              to an IPv6 address.

       AAII__AADDDDRRCCOONNFFIIGG
              This  flag is used with AAFF__IINNEETT66 to further request that queries
              for IPv6 addresses should not be made unless the system  has  at
              least one IPv6 address assigned to a network interface, and that
              queries  for IPv4 addresses should not be made unless the system
              has at least one IPv4 address assigned to a  network  interface.
              This flag may be used by itself or with the AAII__VV44MMAAPPPPEEDD flag.

       AAII__DDEEFFAAUULLTT
